<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amar Sonar Bangla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first published September <a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as আমার সোনার বাংলা</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Amar Sonar Bangla&quot; (&quot;My Golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>&quot;) is an ode to Mother Bengal written by the Bengali poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>. The exact year it was composed is not known, because the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was never found. Its melody is derived from &quot;Ami Kothay Pabo Tare&quot;, a song of the Baul singer Gagan Harkara, set to the rhythm cycle Dadra tala. It is linked with the first Partition of Bengal.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> took effect on 16 October 1905, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>, announced it on 20 July. It split the Bengal Presidency into two parts along communal lines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> having a Muslim majority and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> one, a division claimed to have been politically motivated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> such as this one were meant to rekindle the unified spirit of Bengal and to raise public consciousness against it. Some sources claim the song was written in that context, but the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was never found, so the date of composition cannot be established with certainty.</p><p>Prashanta Kumar Paul, in his biography of Tagore, claimed that it was first sung on 25 August 1905 at an essay conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>. Its lyrics appeared in September 1905 in the journals Bongodorshon and Baul, and the music in notation in the journal Shongeet Biggnan Praveshika. Tagore's niece Indira Devi wrote down the notation as she heard it from Tagore himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The words are an ode to Mother Bengal. Amar means my, and sonar, from sona, gold, is used as a term of endearment, so that Sonar Bangla can express both beloved and precious. The melody derives from Ami Kothay Pabo Tare, a song of the Baul singer Gagan Harkara, set to the rhythm cycle Dadra tala.</p><p>In Syed Ali Ahsan's English translation, the poem speaks of the air like a flute, the scent of mango orchards in Falgun, paddy fields in Ogrohayon, banyan trees along the riverbanks, and a childhood spent in the mother's playhouse with her soil smeared on the body. It closes with the speaker offering his head beneath her feet. Only the first ten lines are sung as the national anthem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Provisional Government of Bangladesh adopted the song in 1971, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a>, and Samar Das composed the orchestral version. When the Constitution of Bangladesh was drafted in 1972, two songs were proposed, this one and Dwijendra Lal Roy's Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara, and Amar Sonar Bangla was selected.</p><p>According to Ziauddin Ahmed, the choice has drawn criticism since at least 1975. Some opponents point out that Bengali Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> had welcomed the partition the song was written against. Others object that the word mother refers to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess. After the coup of 15 August 1975 a committee recommended replacements, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazi-nazrul-islam">Kazi Nazrul Islam</a>'s Notuner Gaan, but the process halted when the president was removed. In 1979 Prime Minister Shah Azizur Rahman proposed Shahnaz Rahmatullah's Prothom Bangladesh instead, as Tagore was not Bangladeshi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amar Sonar Bangla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Amar Sonar Bangla</b>&quot; (&quot;My Golden Bengal&quot;) is an ode to Mother Bengal written by the Bengali poet Rabindranath Tagore.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li><li><blockquote>…the freedom fighters in the Liberation War of Bangladesh, and when the Constitution of Bangladesh was drafted in 1972 it was one of the two songs proposed for the national anthem; Rabindranath Tagore's &quot;<b>Amar Sonar Bangla</b>&quot;, also written in the context of the partition of 1905, was chosen.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dhana-dhanya-pushpa-bhara">Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara</a></li><li><blockquote>It was one of 22 protest songs he wrote in the Swadeshi period of the Indian freedom movement, and with &quot;<b>Amar Sonar Bangla</b>&quot; it became one of the key songs of the movement against the partition of the Bengal Presidency in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ekla-chalo-re">Ekla Chalo Re</a></li></ul></section>
